rebuild their marriage.

00:17:36.354 --> 00:17:37.822
Very adult.
[ Laughter ]

00:17:37.822 --> 00:17:40.425
-Sounds like a kid's movie.
Kids love that stuff.

00:17:40.425 --> 00:17:42.894
-I had to pee within
the first five minutes,

00:17:42.894 --> 00:17:44.996
and it's a very good
Mike Nichols film.

00:17:44.996 --> 00:17:46.265
So, my mom was like,

00:17:46.265 --> 00:17:47.999
"I really don't want to leave,
but I'll take you.

00:17:47.999 --> 00:17:49.034
So then I come back.

00:17:49.034 --> 00:17:50.435
10 minutes later,
I got to pee again,

00:17:50.435 --> 00:17:53.605
and she said, "I'm not taking
you to the bathroom again.

00:17:53.605 --> 00:17:54.907
All right?
I want to watch this movie."

00:17:54.907 --> 00:17:56.841
So, I said, "Okay." I had to go.

00:17:56.841 --> 00:17:59.778
I just waited and I just
went in my jeans and --

00:17:59.778 --> 00:18:00.979
[ Audience gasps ]

00:18:00.979 --> 00:18:03.348
I didn't mind. And then --
[ Laughter ]

00:18:03.348 --> 00:18:06.251
I had a little, you know,
jacket, you know, kid, right?

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:08.387
And I tied it
around my waist in front

00:18:08.387 --> 00:18:10.388
and I walked out, and my mom
said, "What's that about?"

00:18:10.388 --> 00:18:12.724
And I said, "Well, you said
we couldn't get up again.

00:18:12.724 --> 00:18:13.891
So I peed in my jeans."

00:18:13.891 --> 00:18:15.260
-You said that?

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.395
Yeah, and she was like, "What?
Why would you do that?"

00:18:17.395 --> 00:18:18.530
And I was like, "I don't know.

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:20.131
You said you wanted to
watch the movie."

00:18:20.131 --> 00:18:21.232
-Oh, my God.
[ Laughter ]

00:18:21.232 --> 00:18:22.668
What a good kid, man.
-Yeah.

00:18:22.668 --> 00:18:24.402
And also, you know,
I like Mike Nichols, too.

00:18:24.402 --> 00:18:26.371
When I'm 7, I want to see.
[ Laughter ]

00:18:26.371 --> 00:18:27.505
I want to stick around.

00:18:27.505 --> 00:18:29.507
-So, you go into this thing
blind and you --

00:18:29.507 --> 00:18:30.742
they give you a script

00:18:30.742 --> 00:18:32.210
or do you get to improvise
a little bit, or...?

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:33.945
-Oh. So, yes.

00:18:33.945 --> 00:18:37.916
It is wonderfully scripted,
but they did a thing

00:18:37.916 --> 00:18:40.586
that is very generous
when they say to comedy people.

00:18:40.586 --> 00:18:45.057
They say, "Have fun with it and,
you know, add your own words."

00:18:45.057 --> 00:18:46.625
And so,
for the very first session,

00:18:46.625 --> 00:18:47.793
I'm still in fight or flight

00:18:47.793 --> 00:18:48.827
'cause I think
it's a kidnapping.

00:18:48.827 --> 00:18:50.295
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:18:50.295 --> 00:18:55.233
-I did go my own way with it,
and I threw in my own lines,

00:18:55.233 --> 00:18:58.604
and what happened was I was
just cursing wildly.

00:18:58.604 --> 00:19:00.805
[ Laughter ]

00:19:00.805 --> 00:19:02.107
-This is you.

00:19:02.107 --> 00:19:04.676
Thank you for -- you
saved this exclusively for us.

00:19:04.676 --> 00:19:05.877
These are outtakes.

00:19:05.877 --> 00:19:07.179
These are not in the film.

00:19:07.179 --> 00:19:08.713
-Yes, they sent along the video.

00:19:08.713 --> 00:19:12.150
-John Mulaney doing
the voice of Spider-Ham.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:15.120
-This is real. I did this.
-Yeah.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:17.088 line:0%
-Damn right.

00:19:17.088 --> 00:19:19.657 line:0%
Ah, you bastards.

00:19:19.657 --> 00:19:21.693 line:0%
And how the [bleep]
is he gonna do that?

00:19:21.693 --> 00:19:23.095 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:19:23.095 --> 00:19:26.231 line:0%
And how the [bleep]
exactly is he gonna do that?

00:19:26.231 --> 00:19:29.834 line:0%
Two days.

00:19:29.834 --> 00:19:32.236 line:0%
I've been Spider-Ham
30 [bleep] years.

00:19:32.236 --> 00:19:33.372 line:0%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:19:33.372 --> 00:19:37.876
-They can't use any of that?

00:19:37.876 --> 00:19:41.713
When -- At what point
did you find out --

00:19:41.713 --> 00:19:44.750
-They were like --
they had said, "Go nuts."

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:46.585
So I'm like, "You mother--"

00:19:46.585 --> 00:19:48.420
I'm screaming,
and then I paused.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:49.921
I went,
"What is this movie rated?"

00:19:49.921 --> 00:19:52.257
And they said, "PG."

00:19:52.257 --> 00:19:56.427
And I said, "Oh, so, nothing
I've just said is usable."

00:19:56.427 --> 00:19:58.664
And they said, "No, but we just
wanted to you have fun."

00:19:58.664 --> 00:20:00.832
[ Laughter ]

00:20:00.832 --> 00:20:01.933
-Your new Netflix special,

00:20:01.933 --> 00:20:04.136 line:0%
"John Mulaney &amp;
The Sack Lunch Bunch."

00:20:04.136 --> 00:20:06.104 line:0%
-Yes.
-I'm very excited about this.

00:20:06.104 --> 00:20:09.440 line:0%
This is sort of a variety show
with kids involved.

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:16.114 line:0%
-Yes. This is a show for kids,
by an adult, with kids present.

00:20:16.114 --> 00:20:18.950
It's a children's
musical special,

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:22.454
and I am a man with no children
and no musical ability,

00:20:22.454 --> 00:20:24.723
but I wanted
to do a variety special

00:20:24.723 --> 00:20:25.857
and I wanted to do it with kids.

00:20:25.857 --> 00:20:27.225
-Yeah.

00:20:27.225 --> 00:20:30.862
-And kind of -- It's a nod to
the shows I grew up on.

00:20:30.862 --> 00:20:32.297
-Yeah.

00:20:32.297 --> 00:20:35.767
-And I wanted to do like the
"Sesame Street" I grew up on

00:20:35.767 --> 00:20:37.969
and you probably grew up on,
which was that, like, funky,

00:20:37.969 --> 00:20:42.974
like, Art Garfunkel and Grover
and a girl named Gina

00:20:42.974 --> 00:20:45.643
would be, like, leaning against
the stoop, smoking a cigarette.

00:20:45.643 --> 00:20:50.648
And there would be, like, music,
but then someone would come over

00:20:50.648 --> 00:20:52.150
and be like,
"Did you hear about Mr. Hooper?"

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:53.819
And they'd be like, "What?"
They'd be like, "He died."

00:20:53.819 --> 00:20:55.787
And they'd be like "Oh, no.
What's death?"

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:57.322
And they'd be
like, "It's when you die."

00:20:57.322 --> 00:21:01.092
And then they'd just cut to an
industrial video of

00:21:01.092 --> 00:21:04.429
an orange crayon being made,
and we forget about

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:06.931
that we just learned the
existential dread of death

00:21:06.931 --> 00:21:10.068
and we're watching a weird
industrial of

00:21:10.068 --> 00:21:11.903
orange wax becoming crayons.

00:21:11.903 --> 00:21:14.106
And then you cut back,
and it's Smokey Robinson.

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:16.608
And he's like, "Near, far."

00:21:16.608 --> 00:21:18.109
And then it would cut back,
and it was like, "Alright."

00:21:18.109 --> 00:21:20.044
-Yeah, what a weird show.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:21:20.044 --> 00:21:21.246
-It is bizarre.

00:21:21.246 --> 00:21:23.281
-Yeah, I don't think there was
a script to that.

00:21:23.281 --> 00:21:24.683
-No, not at all.

00:21:24.683 --> 00:21:26.951
You start the
whole special with a quote.

00:21:26.951 --> 00:21:29.087
Do you mind...
-Oh, yeah.

00:21:29.087 --> 00:21:31.289
-...reading the quote or saying
the quote to everyone?

00:21:31.289 --> 00:21:34.125
-The special begins
with a maxim that I find

00:21:34.125 --> 00:21:38.263
to be quite true, which is, "Do
you know who tells the truth?"

00:21:38.263 --> 00:21:41.433
"Drunks and children."
-Yeah, that's --

00:21:41.433 --> 00:21:42.634
-Yeah.
-And -- Yeah.

00:21:42.634 --> 00:21:44.302
Whose --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:21:44.302 --> 00:21:46.104
They agree.
-It's true.

00:21:46.104 --> 00:21:49.641
-They agree.
-Wise words. Wise words.

00:21:49.641 --> 00:21:51.309
-Who is that quote from?

00:21:51.309 --> 00:21:53.311
-That is from Erika Jayne of

00:21:53.311 --> 00:21:55.513
"Real Housewives of
Beverly Hills" --

00:21:55.513 --> 00:21:57.649
Season 9, Episode 20.

00:21:57.649 --> 00:22:01.419
It's right after they go to
the wine-tasting thing

00:22:01.419 --> 00:22:04.088
where Kyle and Teddi
get way too drunk.

00:22:04.088 --> 00:22:06.457
The next day, Erika says,
"You know who tells the truth?

00:22:06.457 --> 00:22:07.626
Drunks and children."

00:22:07.626 --> 00:22:09.628
I was working on
the special at the time

00:22:09.628 --> 00:22:11.263
and I was in one of those
moods where I went,

00:22:11.263 --> 00:22:12.264
"That's going in."

00:22:12.264 --> 00:22:13.931
-Yeah. Absolutely.

00:22:13.931 --> 00:22:18.569
-And kids are kind of --
They're not like drunks.

00:22:18.569 --> 00:22:20.271
Well, they can't drive.
They shouldn't drive.

00:22:20.271 --> 00:22:23.441
But they are very blunt
and truthful,

00:22:23.441 --> 00:22:25.610
the way some people are after
they've had a few drinks.

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:27.979
-Yeah.
-They've come up to me on set.

00:22:27.979 --> 00:22:29.947
One kid came up to me the first
day and he said --

00:22:29.947 --> 00:22:32.250
'Cause the special was directed
by a great director,

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:35.487
Rhys Thomas, who
I've worked with many times.

00:22:35.487 --> 00:22:37.288
And he said,
"So, you're not the director?"

00:22:37.288 --> 00:22:38.456
And I said, "No."

00:22:38.456 --> 00:22:39.925
And he said, "Oh."

00:22:39.925 --> 00:22:42.126
And I said, "Do you think I have
less authority now?"

00:22:42.126 --> 00:22:44.629
And the kid said, "Yeah, I do."

00:22:44.629 --> 00:22:46.264
And he walked away.
-Just honest.

00:22:46.264 --> 00:22:47.498
-Yeah, just blunt.

00:22:47.498 --> 00:22:48.533
-But you talk about,
like, some --

00:22:48.533 --> 00:22:49.768
-They got into their fears.
Yeah.

00:22:49.768 --> 00:22:51.036
I wanted to know what
they were worried about.

00:22:51.036 --> 00:22:53.104
-What scared you as a child,
John Mulaney?

00:22:53.104 --> 00:22:54.472
Little John Mulaney.

00:22:54.472 --> 00:22:58.977
-What scared me as a child
was not just Robert De Niro

00:22:58.977 --> 00:23:02.080
from "Cape Fear"
breaking into my house,

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.582
but it was that I,
being the only one

00:23:04.582 --> 00:23:07.085
that was prepared for
Robert De Niro from "Cape Fear"

00:23:07.085 --> 00:23:11.256
to break into our house, that I
would be the lone survivor.

00:23:11.256 --> 00:23:14.593
And so it was on me --
It was my burden,

00:23:14.593 --> 00:23:18.596
because of none -- My brother,
my sisters, my stupid parents --

00:23:18.596 --> 00:23:22.767
they weren't prepared for
Robert De Niro.

00:23:22.767 --> 00:23:25.937
And he kicked
the crap out of Nick Nolte,

00:23:25.937 --> 00:23:27.806
and, Dad, you are no Nick Nolte.

00:23:27.806 --> 00:23:29.607
-Alright, alright.

00:23:29.607 --> 00:23:31.276
-So I had to sleep with --

00:23:31.276 --> 00:23:32.878
I slept with a crowbar
under my bed.

00:23:32.878 --> 00:23:34.112
-What?!
-Yeah, yeah.

00:23:34.112 --> 00:23:35.947
I just found it recently.
It was kind of sweet.

00:23:35.947 --> 00:23:38.983
And...
-It became like a toy of yours?

00:23:38.983 --> 00:23:40.452
-No. It became a crowbar,

00:23:40.452 --> 00:23:43.120
'cause I was ready
to roll at any time.

00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:44.923
And I had a bat,
and it was like,

00:23:44.923 --> 00:23:47.092
"If I hear
Robert De Niro downstairs

00:23:47.092 --> 00:23:48.126
ha-ha-ha-ing it, you know --

00:23:48.126 --> 00:23:50.962
-Yeah. You were ready to attack.
-Yeah.

00:23:50.962 --> 00:23:52.631
-I got to say,
the celebrity cameos

00:23:52.631 --> 00:23:53.798
are fantastic
in this special, as well.

00:23:53.798 --> 00:23:56.801
-Yes, yes.
-It's, like, Richard Kind...

00:23:56.801 --> 00:23:59.303
Yes. Jake Gyllenhaal.

00:23:59.303 --> 00:24:01.940
-Jake Gyllenhaal is in this.
David Byrne.

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:04.108
-Yes,
David Byrne, André De Shields,

00:24:04.108 --> 00:24:05.576
who just won the Tony from
"Hadestown,"

00:24:05.576 --> 00:24:06.744
and Annaleigh Ashford.

00:24:06.744 --> 00:24:08.746
A lot of great Broadway
and film people

00:24:08.746 --> 00:24:10.581
came together,
which was super fun.

00:24:10.581 --> 00:24:12.216
-Is there any guests that
you wanted have on

00:24:12.216 --> 00:24:13.385
that you couldn't get?

00:24:13.385 --> 00:24:15.086
-Stevie Nicks.
-Ooh.

00:24:15.086 --> 00:24:16.921
-I wanted Stevie Nicks.
-She's tough.

00:24:16.921 --> 00:24:18.423
-A hush fell over the room

00:24:18.423 --> 00:24:19.924
unlike any
I've ever heard before.

00:24:19.924 --> 00:24:21.592
-Yeah, 'cause she's
a magical being.

00:24:21.592 --> 00:24:23.762
-She is a magical being,
but she --

00:24:23.762 --> 00:24:27.098
When she passes on a TV project,
she really passes on it.

00:24:27.098 --> 00:24:28.433
-What do you mean?

00:24:28.433 --> 00:24:29.834
-Well, I was looking for her
for like a month.

00:24:29.834 --> 00:24:31.069
You know, I can't catch her.

00:24:31.069 --> 00:24:32.571
You know, she's a ghost
in the wind, right?

00:24:32.571 --> 00:24:33.638
-Yeah.

00:24:33.638 --> 00:24:35.774
-So, I finally called
her manager

00:24:35.774 --> 00:24:37.275
and I said,
"This is John Mulaney."

00:24:37.275 --> 00:24:39.644
And she went,
"Oh, you're that guy."

00:24:39.644 --> 00:24:41.279
And I went, "Yes, I am."

00:24:41.279 --> 00:24:44.616
And she said, "Listen,
Stevie heard about the project,

00:24:44.616 --> 00:24:47.285
but she's on tour
and she has a limited break,

00:24:47.285 --> 00:24:48.953
and so it's just not
something she can do."

00:24:48.953 --> 00:24:50.321
I said, "I totally understand.

00:24:50.321 --> 00:24:52.090
Thank you so much for
considering it."

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:53.491
And then she continued.

00:24:53.491 --> 00:24:56.127
She said, "Also,
Stevie listened to the song,

00:24:56.127 --> 00:24:57.595
and she doesn't think
it's funny."

00:24:57.595 --> 00:24:58.964
And I said, "Oh, okay.

00:24:58.964 --> 00:25:02.233
Yeah, you know, the first answer
was good enough."

00:25:02.233 --> 00:25:05.703
And she said,
"Stevie's confused,

00:25:05.703 --> 00:25:07.339
because this is not
one of her songs."

00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:08.740
And I said, "Oh, no, I know.

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:12.543
It's a parody song that she
would be singing in.

00:25:12.543 --> 00:25:14.913
But if she doesn't like it
and is not available --"

00:25:14.913 --> 00:25:16.714
And she said, "Yeah,
she doesn't like it."

00:25:16.714 --> 00:25:19.217
And I said, "Okay."

00:25:19.217 --> 00:25:21.252
And I started
laughing really hard,

00:25:21.252 --> 00:25:23.921
because this was the longest
pass I'd ever heard,

00:25:23.921 --> 00:25:25.924
in about a decade of
entertainment.

00:25:25.924 --> 00:25:29.427
And she said, "You know, she did
do TV once with Ryan Murphy."

00:25:29.427 --> 00:25:31.596
I said, "Yeah, she was on
'American Horror Story: Coven.'

00:25:31.596 --> 00:25:33.765
She did 'Seven Wonders,'
that song."

00:25:33.765 --> 00:25:35.566
And she said,
"Yes, but 'Seven Wonders'

00:25:35.566 --> 00:25:37.268
is one of Stevie's songs."

00:25:37.268 --> 00:25:41.106
And I said, "I know that.
This was a parody song."

00:25:41.106 --> 00:25:43.274
And she said,
"But she doesn't find it funny."

00:25:43.274 --> 00:25:45.943
And I said, "I get that.
I get all of it."

00:25:45.943 --> 00:25:47.611
-Oh, my God! Wow!

00:25:47.611 --> 00:25:50.281
-I said, "I want to say --
I want to let you know --"

00:25:50.281 --> 00:25:53.318
I said to her,
"I've been passed on many times.

00:25:53.318 --> 00:25:56.788
This is the greatest pass
in my career,

00:25:56.788 --> 00:26:00.725
and please thank Stevie for me
and just tell her I said 'hello'

00:26:00.725 --> 00:26:02.527
and basically tell her I wrote
the part because

00:26:02.527 --> 00:26:04.062
I want to hang out with her."

00:26:04.062 --> 00:26:06.731
And her manager said,
"Well, then come hang out.

00:26:06.731 --> 00:26:09.768
We have people in
the backyard all the time."

00:26:13.538 --> 00:26:15.373
-So, are you going to hang out
with Stevie Nicks?

00:26:15.373 --> 00:26:18.409
-I don't know.
She didn't like my song.

00:26:18.409 --> 00:26:20.378
-Come on, man. Come on.

00:26:20.378 --> 00:26:22.047
She invited you over
to the house.

